findings.body:
Any authenticated caller can set `accountId` in the JSON body and read that account. The previous code always used `caller.accountId` from `auth(req)`; this change substitutes `body.accountId` with no check that the caller is support staff or otherwise authorized for cross-account reads.

Failure mode: a normal user authenticates, sends `{"accountId":"<victim>"}`, and receives the victim account payload from `Response.json(account)`.

Gate cross-account lookup explicitly before the query, for example:

```suggestion
  const body = await req.json().catch(() => ({}));
  const id: string = body.accountId ?? caller.accountId;
  if (id !== caller.accountId && !caller.isSupport) {
    return Response.json({ error: "forbidden" }, { status: 403 });
  }
  const account = await db.accounts.findUnique({ where: { id } });
```

(Adapt `caller.isSupport` to whatever role claim `auth()` actually exposes; the important part is denying cross-account reads by default.)
